protecting groups at the N-terminal of Ala by microwave heating. From $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis of the microwave treated material, we confirmed a $76\%$ yield in the deprotection reaction, as indicated by the down-field shift and integral of the Ala methyl group doublet (from $\delta=1.19$ to 1.33, Figure S34, SI). With similar reactions shown in steps (1) and (2), Bocdeprotections [(3), (5), and (7)], and amino acid loadings [(4), and (6)] were performed to incorporate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}.$ -Pro-Gly-AlaCONHL peptide into the pores of MTV-IRMOF-74-III (Scheme 1). It is worth noting that the maximum number of post-synthetic modifications in tandem previously reported in MOFs is four.2
Powder $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) analyses confirmed that crystallinity and structure remain intact throughout the seven reactions by the coincidence of their profile with that of calculated pattern for unfunctionalized IRMOF-74-III (Figure 1a). The ratio of integrals for the methyl group in $\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-CH}_{3}$ , and for each of the loaded amino acids in the acid digested $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra, was used to quantify the yield of each performed reaction. The tripeptide $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}.$ -Pro-Gly-Ala-CONHL was covalently bound to the MOF with an overall yield of $57\%$ (average of $93\%$ yield per step, Section 2, SI).
After the removal of guest molecules from the pores (Section S1.4, SI), the porosity of the product of the seven postsynthetic reactions, was evaluated by measurement of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . A steep ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake was observed below $P/P_{0}=0.1$ , which is indicative of the presence of permanent porosity even after the seven reactions (Figure 1b). The observed Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of the tripeptide loaded sample $(1760\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is lower than the starting material MTV- $(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{0.6}(\mathrm{CH_{2}N H B o c})_{0.4}$ $(2330~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . This is because of the covalently attached tripeptide, which occupies significant amount of the pore space.
Building complexity in the pores. Encouraged by the successful sequential loading of tripeptide in the MOF pores, we proceeded to covalently bind another peptide sequence of acidic and basic amino acids. To achieve this goal, orthogonal protection of these functionalities is necessary to avoid side reactions. Accordingly, Fluorenylmethyloxycarbonyl (Fmoc) protection and deprotection chemistry9 was used to incorporate the tripeptide $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}.$ -Cys-His-Asp-CONHL into MTV$(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{0.6}(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{0.4}$ . This specific sequence was selected since it has been shown that the amino acids, Asp, His, and Cys (and their proximity in the active site), are responsible for the unique catalytic activity of the enzyme tobacco etch virus (TEV).10 In seven post-synthetic reactions, we prepared MTV$\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{0.6}(C H_{2}N H}$ -Asp-His-Cys- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{0.1}$ , by loading FmocAsp(tBu)-OH, Fmoc-His(Boc)-OH, and Boc-Cys(fm)-OH (fm $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ fluorenylmethyl), where Fmoc or Boc groups were used to protect N-terminal functionalities, and tert-butyl ester $(^{t}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u})$ , Boc, or fm groups to protect the amino acid side chains (in Asp, His, and Cys, respectively). After the Asp and His loadings [steps (2) and (4), Scheme S2, SI], the MOFs were washed with DMF, and the Fmoc groups were removed under basic conditions (Section S2.2, SI). MTV- $\begin{array}{r l}{\cdot(\mathbf{CH}_{3})_{0.6}(\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{NH}\mathbf{.}}\end{array}$ 一 Asp-His-Cys- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{0.1}$ was obtained with an overall yield of $20\%$ (Section S2, SI). $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR and HPLC of the digested samples, were used to follow the reactions (Figures S35 and S44, SI). The presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}$ -Cys-His-Asp-CONHL tripeptide in the pores of IRMOF-74-III was confirmed by the mass of the parent fragment found by ESI-MS analysis of the digested sample (calcd. m/z, $734.2\mathrm{Da}$ ; found, $734.4\mathrm{Da}$ ). PXRD analysis, and ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms at $77~\mathrm{~K~}$ showed that MTV$(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{0.6}(\mathrm{CH_{2}N H\mathrm{-}A s p\mathrm{-}H i s\mathrm{-}C y s\mathrm{-}N H_{2}})_{0.1}$ retained crystallinity, and porosity $\mathrm{(BET=1920~m^{2}~g^{-1})}$ ) after seven post-synthetic covalent reactions (Figures S17 and S41, SI).
Selective catalytic activity of the MTV-MOF. Amino acids and short peptides are known to be active organo-catalysts.11 We chose to evaluate our MTV-IRMOF-III-tripeptides in two different catalytic reactions that strongly depend on the complexity of the pores. MTV- $\mathrm{\cdot(CH_{3})_{0.6}(C H_{2}N H\mathrm{-}A l a\mathrm{-}G l y\mathrm{-}P r o\mathrm{-}N H_{2})_{0.2}}$ was evaluated as catalyst in the $\mathfrak{a}$ -chlorination of butyraldehyde which is known to be catalyzed by proline derivatives [literature: $99\%$ conv, $2\%$ enantioselectivity (ee)].12 We sought to take advantage of the molecular control in the pores of IRMOF-74-III, to study the effect of pore constrains on this reaction. Hence, we prepared MTV- $(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{0.8}(\mathrm{CH_{2}N H\mathrm{-Pro\mathrm{-}N H_{2}})_{0.2}}$ (Cat. A, Scheme S4, SI), as a control catalyst and compared its activity with MTV$\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{0.6}(C H_{2}N H}$ -Ala-Gly-Pro-NH2)0.2 (Cat. B). ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms for both materials showed a $17\%$ decrease in the BET surface area of Cat. B compared to Cat. A $(1760~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $2120~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, Section S7, SI). This decrease is related to the bulkiness of the tripeptide $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}$ -Pro-Gly-Ala-CONHL), compared to a simple amino acid Pro in the pores. Under the same conditions Cat. A was found to provide $69\%$ more of the desired product compared to Cat. B ( $94\%$ vs. $25\%$ , Figure S48, SI). This is expected since there is less available pore volume in Cat. B.
Notably, a significant increase in the ee of this $\mathfrak{a}$ -chlorination reaction was observed when using functionalized MOF as catalysts compared to the reported homogeneous phase proline catalysis $20\%$ ee vs. $2\%$ ee). This increased asymmetric induction, which is likely due to the increased stereochemical constraints within the congested MOF framework,13 is especially striking given that the catalytic sites are not entirely uniform (Scheme 1). Ultimately, these results demonstrate the exciting potential of imparting control of catalyst reactivity and selectivity by the molecular tuning of the MOF architecture and the active site.
Next, we turned our attention to recreating the high selectivity exhibited by the enzyme TEV endopeptidase within a synthetically modified MOF framework. Specifically, MTV$\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{0.6}(C H_{2}N H\mathrm{-Asp\mathrm{-}H i s\mathrm{-}C y s\mathrm{-}N H_{2})_{0.1}}}$ was studied as a heterogeneous catalyst in sequence-specific peptide cleavage due to its TEV-like peptide sequence (Figure 2a).
TEV protease is known to recognize selectively the amino acid serine (Ser) in a peptide substrate, and cleave the amide bond in which Ser is involved. In order to achieve such precision, three amino acids in its active site (Asp, His, and Cys/Ser), have been proposed to participate cooperatively (Figure 2b). It is believed that the acid polarizes His, which simultaneously activates the nucleophile (Cys) for attack on
